{
	title: 'Keep generated trees out of change tracking',
	spec: `Invariant: automatic file-change bookkeeping scales with useful project edits, not dependency trees or generated output. Replace the ef1 default that every declared file beneath cwd or /tmp is worth tracking. Exclude node_modules and .git trees and Git-ignored untracked files from automatic snapshots, change records, changed-path sets and related reporting. Tracked files remain eligible even when an ignore rule matches. Use native Git ignore rules where available, batch checks, and prune excluded directories before recursive enumeration; do not enumerate a whole dependency tree merely to discard it. Apply lexical and canonical exclusions. File access and command execution remain unrestricted by tracking policy; preserve WRITE/EDIT safety locks, existing eligible-file concurrency protection, sensitive-file handling and independent commit notices. A hook can override the tracking default.

Existing histories must benefit too: provide a bounded-memory, explicit cleanup of excluded file_changes entries, preserving all conversation records, useful change entries, record numbers and referenced snapshots. Before replacement, retain a recoverable original and validate the replacement; coordinate with the host so concurrent appends cannot be lost, atomically replace and rebuild affected byte-offset marks, and invalidate history, changes and changed-path caches. Leave snapshot-blob garbage collection out of scope. Do not silently rewrite history on startup or delete data without approval. Until cleanup, do not claim the old memory problem is fixed.

Prefer one shared eligibility predicate and existing history/migration facilities. No new cache framework, database, sidecar audit system, periodic GC or memory-budget tuning. Terminal and web consume the same filtered host data; no layout changes. Verify ordinary tracked and new unignored file diffs still work, ignored/generated trees produce no per-file tracking, tool access and locking remain correct, and cleanup is lossless outside excluded metadata and recoverable on failure. Compare host RSS and post-GC heap before and after an equivalent large generated-tree workload and after loading a cleaned legacy history; report measurements rather than promising a fixed saving.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Implementation: file-tracking.ts supplies shared lexical/canonical exclusions and batched native git check-ignore (tracked ignored files remain eligible). Git-aware globs use ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard plus the overridable generatedDirs exclusions so dependency and ignored trees are pruned before enumeration; non-Git globs walk directories with the same dependency exclusions. file-changes.ts applies eligibility before snapshots/declaration locks/notices and rechecks after execution. WRITE/EDIT retain their literal safety locks. Bash help describes the default.',
		'Legacy cleanup is deliberately offline rather than a live slash command: bun src/host/change-cleanup.ts <session-id> previews; --apply explicitly approves metadata removal. Stop host, takeover clients and supervisor first. The existing exclusive home lock protects the entire operation; a fresh host inherently reloads history/changes/path caches. Cleanup holds one record at a time, preserves all record types and original numbers (materializing old offset-based numbers), validates the candidate, retains an fsynced exclusive 0600 original backup, detects source changes, atomically replaces history and rebuilds offset marks. Malformed/torn input, symlinks, backup failures and candidate corruption abort without replacing history. Snapshot blobs are untouched. No real histories have been cleaned; restart and explicit cleanup remain necessary to reduce an existing hosts retained history.',
		'Synthetic before/after at the baseline task-spec revision versus implementation: 3,000 generated files changed over eight rounds plus one source file; captured entries 24,008 -> 8; history 4,409,560 -> 2,440 bytes; workload 14,359 -> 1,043 ms; post-GC RSS 111,800,320 -> 60,608,512 bytes; JSC heap 12,038,724 -> 6,732,388 bytes. Separate disposable 50,000-generated-entry legacy cleanup preserved all three records and one useful change: 6,389,278 -> 414 bytes in 346 ms. Fresh-process readSync + changes.list + GC before/after cleanup: RSS 172,462,080 -> 38,916,096 bytes; process heapUsed 49,217,413 -> 2,234,150 bytes. These are synthetic measurements, not production-memory promises.',
		'Final focused verification: 29 tests across capture, Actions, neighbors, cleanup and changes pass; changed-file lint has zero warnings/errors. Project typecheck fails only replay.test.ts:201 invalid inbox/replaces fields, reproduced at the unchanged baseline. Full ./test failed existing source line-limit checks and e2e/web tests, then timed out after 120 seconds; it is not green. No flaky test was retried or timeout extended. Conventions import side-effect checks passed. Cleanup --help runs. Existing failing areas and concurrent UI work were left untouched.',
		'Production cleanup initially aborted without replacing history because git check-ignore rejects descendant pathspecs beyond symlinks. Invariant restored: lexical ignored symlink ancestors and canonical target ignores are checked without passing Git a path beyond a symlink. gitPath truncates divergent aliases to their first symlink ancestor; a regression covers useful external aliases and ignored aliases. Cleanup shutdown coordination must recognize a zombie child while its restart-loop parent is paused; slow host shutdown may exceed two minutes.'
	],
	needs: ['ef1', 'fz'],
	foldInto: ['ef1', 'fz']
}
